<CachingArticle slug="">
 <p>A visitor opens the page for a blue mug. Our application reads the catalogue, finds its name and price, and assembles the response. A moment later another visitor opens the same page. The application does the same work again.</p>
 <p>Nothing about the mug has changed. We could have kept the first answer.</p>
 <p>Keeping an answer so that a later request can reuse it is <strong>caching</strong>. The saved answer is a <strong>cache entry</strong>. It might live in the application's own memory, in the visitor's browser, or in another service. We will use Redis, a server that can hold values in memory and retrieve them by a key. Our application will ask it for a product response before going back to the catalogue.</p>
 <p>Let's follow one request. Press <strong>Play request</strong> below. The dot carries a message between the reader, our application and the two places that can hold the price. Then choose <strong>Later request</strong> and play it again.</p>
 <FirstCopy kind="journey" />
 <p>On the first trip, Redis has nothing to return. The application still has to read the catalogue. On the second, the catalogue stays quiet. The answer takes a shorter route because an earlier request has already paid for the work.</p>

 <section>
  <h2>A place to put the answer</h2>
  <p>We need some way to find that saved response again. A Redis entry has a <strong>key</strong>, which identifies it, and a <strong>value</strong>, which holds its contents. For this page we might choose <code>product:blue-mug:EUR</code> as the key and save a small JSON value:</p>
  <pre><code>{'{"name":"Blue mug","price":18,"currency":"EUR"}'}</code></pre>
  <p>Redis can store those bytes as a string. Here, “string” describes a sequence of bytes; it doesn't require the contents to be a human sentence. Our application decides that these bytes are JSON, writes them in that format and decodes them when reading. Redis doesn't infer a relationship between this key and a row in our catalogue.</p>
  <p>A <code>GET</code> command asks Redis for the value under a key. If the string is there, Redis returns it: we have a <strong>hit</strong>. If the key is absent, Redis returns a null reply: we have a <strong>miss</strong>. The command doesn't know where to get a missing answer. In our arrangement the application makes that decision.</p>
  <p>After a miss, the application reads the catalogue and builds the response. It uses <code>SET</code> to save the response under the key, then returns it to the visitor. This arrangement is usually called <strong>cache-aside</strong>: the application manages a cache alongside its ordinary source of data. The two outgoing paths in the illustration belong to the application.</p>
  <p>Notice that the first request became more expensive. It added a cache lookup and a cache write to the original catalogue work. We are making an investment in later requests. Whether it pays off depends on how many of those requests can use what we saved.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>Traffic is not the same as reuse</h2>
  <p>Imagine twenty-four visitors arriving one at a time. They might all want the same mug. They might want twenty-four different products. The number of visitors is the same, but the opportunity to reuse an answer is quite different.</p>
  <p>In the next illustration a number identifies a product. With four different products, the requests cycle through products 1, 2, 3 and 4. Play the requests, or move the lower slider to the end. Then increase <strong>Different products</strong> to twenty-four.</p>
  <FirstCopy kind="reuse" />
  <p>With four products, the first row does all four catalogue reads. Once we have saved the four answers, the remaining requests find their copies. Twenty-four requests produce four catalogue reads and twenty hits. With twenty-four products, every request asks a new question. We save twenty-four answers without using any of them again.</p>
  <p>The <strong>hit rate</strong> is the fraction of lookups that find an entry. In the four-product example it is 20 ÷ 24, or about 83%. In the twenty-four-product example it is zero. Both workloads send exactly the same number of requests.</p>
  <p>This tendency to return to a small set of answers is a form of <strong>locality</strong>. A popular product page has it. A report that every user runs once with a unique set of filters may have very little. We should look for repeated questions in the workload, rather than assuming that a busy service must benefit from a cache.</p>
  <p>Our boxes also reveal why a cache starts <strong>cold</strong>. An empty cache has no past work to reuse. As requests fill it, it becomes <strong>warm</strong>. The hit rate above includes that initial work. If we measured only the later requests for the same four products, we would report 100%. Neither number is dishonest, but they describe different portions of the workload. A measurement that ignores restarts and newly popular products can hide a substantial amount of work.</p>
  <p>For now every copy fits and lasts indefinitely. Real caches have limited space and answers that stop being usable. Each disappearance creates another first request. We will return to both limits after understanding what a hit actually saves.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>How much shorter is the shortcut?</h2>
  <p>A database can already be fast. An index may locate the mug immediately, and the database may have the necessary pages in memory. Putting a network cache in front of it doesn't remove all work: we still send a request to Redis, wait for a reply and decode it.</p>
  <p>Suppose that retrieving and decoding a cached answer takes 2 milliseconds. Reading the catalogue and assembling an answer takes 20 milliseconds. Saving a new copy takes another 2 milliseconds, and our application waits for that save before replying. A hit takes 2 milliseconds; a miss takes 24. Without the cache every request takes 20.</p>
  <p>Move the hit count below. The two paths keep their lengths, but their contribution to the average changes. Then reduce the source duration to 1 millisecond and see whether even a perfect hit rate helps.</p>
  <FirstCopy kind="cost" />
  <p>At ninety hits in a hundred requests, ninety requests take 2 milliseconds and ten take 24. Their total is 420 milliseconds, so the mean is 4.2 milliseconds per request. The catalogue also performs only ten reads instead of a hundred. We have improved both average response time and the amount of work reaching the source.</p>
  <p>At zero hits, every request takes the longer route. And when the source takes only 1 millisecond, the cache lookup alone costs more than the entire original path. Reusing an answer saves catalogue work in that case, but makes the response slower. These are separate benefits, and we should measure the one we need.</p>
  <p>We can express the same calculation for any hit rate. Let <var>h</var> be the fraction of hits, <var>C</var> the cache lookup time, <var>D</var> the original source path, and <var>F</var> the time to fill the cache. Every request pays <var>C</var>; only misses pay <var>D + F</var>:</p>
  <p class="equation">mean = C + (1 − h)(D + F)</p>
  <p>To improve on the original <var>D</var>, we need <var>C + (1 − h)(D + F) &lt; D</var>. Rearranging gives <var>h &gt; (C + F) / (D + F)</var>. With our 2, 20 and 2 millisecond paths, the break-even hit rate is 4 ÷ 22, about 18.2%. This is a result of the model, not a target to carry into production.</p>
  <p>Real requests wait in queues, share connections and transfer values of different sizes. A cache can make the source's remaining queries faster by relieving contention. It can also introduce its own congestion. We would need measurements to know which effect dominates.</p>
  <p>Even in our simple model, the average conceals the slow path. At a 90% hit rate, one request in ten still takes 24 milliseconds. A good average doesn't mean that almost every visitor gets a 4.2 millisecond response. Measure the distribution of response times, including the slow end, as well as the average.</p>
  <p>Hit rate also counts cheap and expensive answers equally. Saving a thousand tiny lookups may matter less than saving one expensive report. Alongside hits, measure the source work avoided, the time spent on each path and the size of the values transferred. These measurements tell us whether the shortcut is useful.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>A copy can disagree</h2>
  <p>So far the catalogue has stood still. Suppose the shop reduces the mug's price from €18 to €15. The catalogue changes, but the bytes we saved in Redis remain exactly as they were. A perfectly successful <code>GET</code> can now produce an out-of-date answer.</p>
  <p>Move time past five seconds in the illustration below. The catalogue changes while the copy stays still. At ten seconds, the copy reaches its expiry time. Try the same sequence with <strong>Delete the copy when the source changes</strong> selected.</p>
  <FirstCopy kind="freshness" />
  <p>An out-of-date copy is <strong>stale</strong>. It is still a hit: the key exists and Redis can return it. Hit rate measures presence, so it cannot by itself tell us whether the answer is suitable.</p>
  <p>There are two different ways the copy disappears in our illustration. Giving it a <strong>time to live</strong>, or TTL, sets a deadline after which it is no longer available. Removing it because we know its source has changed is <strong>invalidation</strong>. In Redis, <code>DEL</code> can remove a key. Neither action fetches a new price. The next request must discover the absence and rebuild the answer.</p>
  <p>The ten-second TTL starts when the copy is saved. An ordinary <code>GET</code> does not renew it. If the price changes just before expiry, the stale interval is short; if it changes just after the fill, that interval can be almost the whole lifetime.</p>
  <p>We have quietly assumed that a newly saved copy starts with current data. That assumption can fail. A slow reader might obtain €18, pause while the price changes, then save its old answer with a brand-new TTL. A ten-second lifetime then measures ten seconds since the save, not ten seconds since the information was current. Freshness requires reasoning about the source read as well as the cache clock.</p>
  <p>Our optional deletion also hides a gap. A catalogue update and a Redis deletion are two operations in different systems. The application can fail between them, or another request can refill an old value after the deletion. We will examine those histories in detail in the freshness article. For now, the important observation is visible in the two boxes: changing one does not automatically change the other.</p>
  <p>Whether that disagreement is acceptable depends on what the answer is used for. A product page might tolerate a briefly outdated display. At checkout, the operation that commits the sale must establish the payable price and reserve the stock under the application's authoritative rules. A cached “one left” is a description of an earlier observation; it cannot reserve the last mug.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>Try the copy in Redis</h2>
  <p>We can reproduce the basic conversation with a real server. If you have Docker running, the following command starts a disposable Redis instance. It publishes no network port and saves no persistent data. The pinned version makes the example repeatable; it is not a claim about the newest release.</p>
  <pre><code>{String.raw`docker run --rm -d --name caching-first redis:8.2.2 \
  redis-server --save "" --appendonly no
docker exec -it caching-first redis-cli`}</code></pre>
  <p>The second command opens Redis's command-line client inside that container. Enter the commands below one at a time. Lines beginning with <code>&gt;</code> are commands; the other lines show their replies. The catalogue read is represented by the value we supply to <code>SET</code>; there is no catalogue database running in this exercise.</p>
  <pre><code>{String.raw`> GET product:blue-mug:EUR
(nil)
> SET product:blue-mug:EUR '{"name":"Blue mug","price":18,"currency":"EUR"}' EX 60
OK
> GET product:blue-mug:EUR
"{\"name\":\"Blue mug\",\"price\":18,\"currency\":\"EUR\"}"
> TTL product:blue-mug:EUR
(integer) 57`}</code></pre>
  <p><code>EX 60</code> puts the value and its sixty-second expiry into the same <code>SET</code> command. <code>TTL</code> reports the remaining whole seconds, so your number depends on how quickly you type. If sixty seconds have already passed, <code>GET</code> returns <code>(nil)</code> again and <code>TTL</code> returns <code>-2</code>, meaning the key is absent. A TTL result of <code>-1</code> instead means that the key exists without an expiry.</p>
  <p>To invalidate the copy before it expires, enter <code>DEL product:blue-mug:EUR</code>, then repeat <code>GET</code>. A deletion reply of <code>1</code> means one key was removed; <code>0</code> means it was already absent. The following lookup misses either way. Redis has not gone looking for a newer mug price.</p>
  <p>Now fill it with a €15 answer and read it back. You are performing the application's job: choosing the key, obtaining a value and deciding when to save it. When finished, leave the client with <code>QUIT</code> and stop only this exercise's container:</p>
  <pre><code>docker stop caching-first</code></pre>
  <p>These command behaviours were checked against Redis 8.2.2. The animated figures are separate teaching models; their invented timings are not benchmark results from this exercise.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>What we have added to the application</h2>
  <p>We began with one path from a request to its answer. We now have two paths and two copies. The shorter path saves work when requests repeat, but it also introduces a network dependency, memory consumption and the possibility of disagreement.</p>
  <p>Because the catalogue retains the authoritative record, losing our cached product response need not lose the product. It does, however, bring back the work. If the cache usually absorbs most requests, an empty or unavailable cache can send far more traffic to the catalogue than it normally handles. “We can rebuild it” and “we can rebuild it quickly enough” are different claims.</p>
  <p>Redis can also hold information that cannot be reconstructed elsewhere. An application might put its only copy of a session or a counter there. The software is the same, but the responsibility has changed. Losing that entry is no longer just losing a shortcut. Later articles will separate those uses and the recovery guarantees they need.</p>
  <p>Before adding a cache to an application, find an answer that is costly to produce, requested repeatedly and safe to reuse for an explicit period or under an explicit rule. Measure the original path. Then measure the hit, miss and failure paths with the cache in place. If the original lookup is already cheap, improving that path or keeping a small local copy may be a better choice than introducing a shared service.</p>
  <p>This collection will develop the other decisions from the mechanisms that require them: which requests may share a key, how copies stay useful as data changes, what happens when many requests miss together, and how Redis behaves under load and failure. The next question is already hiding in our key. If two visitors should see different prices, can they safely ask for the same saved answer?</p>
 </section>
